    "faq_items": {
      "q1": "What is Mosca Tee?",
      "a1": "Mosca Tee is a professional online graphic editor. It was created to be a free alternative to private tools.",
      "q2": "Is it really free?",
      "a2": "Yes, 100%. There are no premium plans, subscriptions or locked features. All tools, including AI background removal and vectorization, are free for everyone.",
      "q3": "Do I need to login to use it?",
      "a3": "No. We believe in agility. You open the site and start creating right away. We don't ask for your email or any other personal data.",
      "q4": "Where are my files saved?",
      "a4": "For privacy reasons, Mosca Tee processes everything locally. Your files are not sent to our servers. You must export your work to save it to your device.",
      "q5": "Can I use the designs commercially?",
      "a5": "Yes! Everything you create in Mosca Tee is yours. You can use your art for commercial purposes, social networks, prints or any other project without restrictions.",
      "q6": "How does Mosca Tee work for blind or low-vision people?",
      "a6": "Mosca Tee is the world's first graphic editor developed with real accessibility for blind and low-vision people. Unlike other tools that only \"support\" screen readers, we built a complete experience from scratch: a numbered grid system on the canvas, like a chessboard, which allows the user to know exactly where each element is positioned. With dedicated shortcuts, reading of icons and images before download, and fully guided navigation, anyone can create professional designs with autonomy and precision.",
      "q7": "What features does Mosca Tee offer for blind and low-vision people?",
      "a7": "Mosca Tee's accessibility system is robust and grows with every update. Check out what's already available:\n\nF1 – Canvas description: hear a general description of what is present in the creation area.\nF3 – Element description: informs dimensions, type and details of the selected object.\nF6 – Object listing: announces all objects present on the canvas in order.\nF9 – Object position: hear the exact position (X and Y) of the selected element.\nNumbered grid on the canvas: the sides have numbers and the base has letters, functioning like a chessboard-style diagram, so the user knows exactly where each object is.\nImage and icon library reader: before inserting or downloading any image or icon, the system describes the content.\nKeyboard guided navigation: dedicated shortcuts for all main functions.\n\nAnd this is just the beginning; new accessibility features are continuously added."
    },
    "cta": {
      "line1": "We can save you when you're without Photoshop,",
      "line2": "but you run a serious risk of liking it."
    },
